.TH XKBSEL 5 "23 Jul 1999" "xkbsel" ""
.\" $Id: xkbsel.5,v 1.2 1999/07/23 14:46:32 stano Exp $
.SH "نام (NAME)"
xkbsel \- XKB keyboard selection tool files

.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
.B xkbsel
is a framework for defining, selecting and
indicating XKB keyboard mappings. It allows
to attach additional data to the XKB source
files and to select (per-system and per-user)
the subset of the mappings presented to the
user in a GUI or command-line interface.
.PP
This manual page describes the syntax of
the configuration files and the extensions
of the XKB syntax used for attaching the
additional data.
.SH "فایل‌های پیکربندی (CONFIGURATION FILES)"
The configuration files are named
.I xkbsel.conf
and contain configuration entries divided
into sections. Section names are enclosed
in square brackets and the entries can
be qualified by an optional qualifier.
An entry can have zero or more values:
.RS
.PP
# comment
.br
// comment
.br
[section]
.br
entry1 = value
.br
entry2[qualif] = val1 "special-val2" val3
.RE
.PP
The section names, entry names, qualifiers and
unquoted values start with letter, slash
or underscore; the remaining characters
can include numbers and parentheses.
Anything other in the value part must be quoted
(there is no possibility yet to get the double
quotes in the value itself). The files are generally
case-sensitive and the ordering of the sections
matters.
.PP
If the user configuration file exist, it is
used as a whole - no merging with system
configuration file occurs.
.PP
Presently there are two sections:
.SS shortcut
Shortcuts define a mapping between short names
used for convenient naming of maps in command-line
and GUI tools and full XKB keymap names. The XKB
keymap name consists of a relative path to a file
(often there is no directory part) and a name
of a map in this particular file in parentheses.
.PP
An example of a shortcut section defining american,
german and french keyboards (from the X11 distribution)
and slovak one (from the
.B xkbsel
distribution) is:
.RS
.PP
[shortcut]
.br
us = xfree86(us)
.br
de = xfree86(de)
.br
fr = xfree86(fr)
.br
sk = czsk(sk)
.RE
.SS mapset
The shortcuts defined in the previous section are used to build
an ordered selection list, used by GUI selectors to present
a menu of choices to the user. There is a possibility
to define lists according to language part of the locale
definition (in small letters), territory part (in caps)
and/or a default list when no specific list matches.
An example:
.RS
.PP
[mapset]
.br
map[sk] = sk us
.br
map[de] = de us
.br
map[fr] = fr us
.br
map = us sk de fr
.RE
.SS ADDITIONAL XKB DATA
It is assumed you know how to create a XKB
keyboard mapping. This section only explains how to
add the
.B xkbsel
data to the new mappings and to the mappings
already present in the X distribution.
.PP
The
.B xkbsel
package mirrors the XKB directory structure (normally
residing in \fI/usr/X11R6/lib/X11/xkb\fR)
in the \fBxkbsel\fR's system directory (normally
\fI/usr/share/xkbsel\fR) and eventually
user's directory (\fI~/.xkbsel\fR).
.PP
The only directory from the original XKB structure
that matters for \fBxkbsel\fR is the \fIkeymap\fR
subdirectory.  This is the starting point where
the package searches for the mappings and passes
them for compilation and installation to the
\fBxkbcomp\fR(1).
.PP
\fBxkbsel\fR adds another two directories to the
hierarchy. \fIredef\fR contains files that add
additional \fBxkbsel\fR data to the mappings
already defined, without needing to change
the mappings themselves in any way. \fIpixmap\fR
contains small icons that can be referenced
by the GUI tools (this is not yet implemented).
.PP
When there are conflicting data, the priority
from the highest to lowest is:
.RS
1. \fIredef\fR directory in the user hierarchy
.br
2. files from the user hierarchy
.br
3. \fIredef\fR directory in the \fBxkbsel\fR hierarchy
.br
4. files from the \fBxkbsel\fR hierarchy
.br
5. original X11 distribution
.RE
The files from the user's directories other than
\fIkeymap\fR or \fIredef\fR (e.g. \fIsymbols\fR)
are only used when the mapping itself is mentioned
in some file in these two directories - otherwise
the \fBxkbsel\fR does not know about them.
.PP
The data for \fBxkbsel\fR is added to the XKB
files using specially formatted comments
in the scope of the particular keymap definition.
The format of the comment is
.RS
\fB%xkb_\fIkeyword\fB%\fR \fB"\fIvalue\fB"\fR
.RE
Presently there are two such data entries:
.TP
.B description
defines a description of the keyboard mapping
in the GUI tools
.TP
.B pixmap
defines a pixmap for the GUI tools (not yet implemented)
.PP
An example of such keymap definition is:
.RS
xkb_keymap "sk" {
.br
    // %xkb_description% "Slovenska klavesnica"
.br
    // %xkb_pixmap% "slovak.xpm"
.br
    xkb_keycodes       { include "xfree86"      };
.br
    xkb_types          { include "default"      };
.br
    xkb_compatibility  { include "default"      };
.br
    xkb_symbols        { include "czsk_ibm(sk)" };
.br
    xkb_geometry       { include "pc"           };
.br
};
.RE
The descriptions can use characters in a local character
encoding, but remember that a GUI tool runs in a specific
locale and won't neccessarily display all selected
map descriptions correctly.
.PP
It is possible to add these data into existing
mappings. As it is practical to do this only in several
files instead of matching redefinition files to
keymap files, there is a possibility to define
file name for the following definitions. This works
only for files in \fIredef\fR directories and is
specified as
.RS
\fB%xkb_file% "\fIvalue\fB"\fR
.RE
outside of keymap definitions. So you can do something
like this:
.RS
// %xkb_file% "xfree86"
.br
xkb_keymap "us"
.br
{
.br
  // %xkb_description% "US layout"
.br
  // %xkb_pixmap% "us.xpm"
.br
};
.br

.br
// %xkb_file% "sun/xfree86"
.br
xkb_keymap "type4_us"
.br
{
.br
  // %xkb_description% "US layout for Sun"
.br
  // %xkb_pixmap% "us.xpm"
.br
};
.RE
.SH "فایل‌ها (FILES)"
.TP
.B /usr/X11R6/lib/X11/xkb
XKB hierarchy of the X11 distribution
.TP
.B /usr/share/xkbsel
system data directory
.TP
.B /etc/xkbsel.conf
system configuration file
.TP
.B ~/.xkbsel
user data directory
.TP
.B ~/.xkbsel/xkbsel.conf
user\'s configuration file
.SH "همچنین ببینید (SEE ALSO)"
.BR xkbseldb (1),
